{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And the hands of Moses.</b> We know that a man has no strength to raise his hands a whole day, and not even a few hours, much less until the sun comes. Now Scripture mentions 'the hands of Moses grew heavy' — heavier than the hands of the rest of the elders; therefore he could not raise them constantly." "And the sense of 'and they placed' is that he could not stand, and they supported him in place." "And Aaron and Hur stood. Scripture says 'heavy' in the masculine plural, and thus one on this side and one on that side; like it: 'and behold, a hand was sent to me, and in it a scroll' (Ezekiel 2:9)." "<b>And his hands were faith.</b> Each of his hands, like 'the daughters of steps' (Genesis 49:22), and like 'the righteous trust like a lion' (Proverbs 28[:1])." "And 'faith' is a thing standing and established, and it is a noun. And some say it is from the root of 'and he became foster-father to Hadassah' (Esther 2:7), as though they were supporters who would carry his hands."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {2 "'כבדים על לשון זכרים' is difficult; the point seems to be that כבדים is grammically masculine plural, applied to hands, which are also masculine; the comparison to Ezekiel illustrates 'in it/with it' referring to a dual object held by two parties.", 3 "Ibn Ezra is arguing that ידיו אמונה means 'his hands [were] steady,' with אמונה as an adjective; his two prooftexts illustrate a singular noun functioning distributively over a plural subject."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:26.472661264Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}